        "headingId": 955,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-06-09T11:31:22"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 991,
    "bookid": 72,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 956,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 150124,
        "bookId": 72,
        "chapterId": 175,
        "headingId": 956,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>These lesions virtually always arise in the colon, and are usually left-sided {19065103;16160473}. Origin in the upper GI tract and bladder has been described {16160473;32460039}.</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-06-09T11:31:22"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 992,
    "bookid": 72,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 957,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 150125,
        "bookId": 72,
        "chapterId": 175,
        "headingId": 957,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>Almost all cases are detected incidentally during endoscopy, for screening or other reasons.</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-06-09T11:31:22"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 993,
    "bookid": 72,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 958,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 150126,
        "bookId": 72,
        "chapterId": 175,
        "headingId": 958,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>Schwann cell hamartoma is an uncommon lesion with cases describing tumours usually occurring in older adults (sixth to seventh decades of life), with a female predominance {32460039}.</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-06-09T11:31:22"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 994,
    "bookid": 72,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 959,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 150127,
        "bookId": 72,
        "chapterId": 175,
        "headingId": 959,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>There are no known syndromic associations.</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-06-09T11:31:22"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 995,
    "bookid": 72,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 960,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 150128,
        "bookId": 72,
        "chapterId": 175,
        "headingId": 960,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>The pathogenesis is unknown. It is not known whether these lesions are neoplastic.</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-06-09T11:31:22"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1002,
    "bookid": 72,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 967,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 150129,
        "bookId": 72,
        "chapterId": 175,
        "headingId": 967,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>These are small polypoid or sessile lesions, typically &lt;&nbsp;10&nbsp;mm and averaging 2&nbsp;mm. The overlying mucosa is usually intact.</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-06-09T11:31:22"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 996,
    "bookid": 72,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 961,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 150130,
        "bookId": 72,
        "chapterId": 175,
        "headingId": 961,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>Schwann cell hamartoma is an intramucosal lesion and may be circumscribed or have infiltrative edges {19065103;16160473;32460039}. It is composed of a pure population of bland Schwann cells that are spindled, have wavy nuclei, inconspicuous nucleoli, and palely eosinophilic cytoplasm. The cells typically entrap colonic crypts. There is at most mild cytological pleomorphism, and mitoses are typically absent. In contrast to conventional gastrointestinal schwannoma, Schwann cell hamartoma lacks peritumoural lymphoid aggregates, dense collagenous stroma, and nuclear pleomorphism.</p>\r\n<p>Schwann cell hamartomas should be distinguished from mucosal benign epithelioid nerve sheath tumours {16160473}, which are essentially the same as epithelioid schwannoma of the soft tissues (see <a style=\"cursor:pointer\" href=\"/chaptercontent/72/174\" target=\"_blank\">section </a><em>Schwannoma</em>).</p>\r\n<p>The tumour cells of both subtypes show diffuse positivity for S100 and SOX10, and are negative for CD34, EMA, KIT, DOG1 and desmin.</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-06-09T11:31:22"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 997,
    "bookid": 72,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 962,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 150131,
        "bookId": 72,
        "chapterId": 175,
        "headingId": 962,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-06-09T11:31:22"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 998,
    "bookid": 72,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 963,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 150132,
